  "narrative": "During the 1970s, Black and African women's hairstyles and hair culture were marked by a growing emphasis on naturalness and self-expression. The afro, a hairstyle created by combing out natural growth of afro-textured hair, was a defining feature of the decade, with its distinctive curl pattern and rounded shape evoking a sense of pride and identity among African American women. Other hairstyles popular during this time included cornrows, which were often worn in simple, straight lines or more elaborate geometric designs, as well as locs (also known as dreadlocks), which could form naturally in very curly hair or be created through techniques like twisting or backcombing. While the source material does not provide specific information about hairstyles during this decade, it is clear that a growing movement towards natural and traditional hairstyles was underway, reflecting a desire among Black and African women to reconnect with their cultural heritage and express themselves authentically. Unfortunately, the available text does not provide more detailed insights into hair culture during the 1970s, but it suggests that this period marked an important shift towards embracing and celebrating natural beauty.",
